http://blog.myspace.com/index.cfm?fuseaction=3Dblog.view&amp;friendID=3D778=
51792&amp;blogID=3D130868121&amp;Mytoken=3D26C6508D-0CAC-43BB-BDA1B437DCB0A=
A7811819937</a>
is a candid transcript of one such occasion), but it's remarkably
non-productive.</span></font><o:p></o:p></p>

<p><font size=3D2 face=3DArial><span style=3D'font-size:10.0pt;font-family:=
Arial'>&nbsp;</span></font><o:p></o:p></p>

<p><font size=3D2 face=3DArial><span style=3D'font-size:10.0pt;font-family:=
Arial'>Of
course, the other dream is to actually teach a MI how to speak and understa=
nd
lojban.&nbsp; Assuming such an auto-associative system could be made hardwa=
re
wise, there would be absolutely nothing preventing it from being a perfectl=
y
natural feeling interface in spoken or typed lojban.&nbsp; You could ask it
absurd questions and it could give you obviously considered answers.&nbsp; =
The
problem I find with this is that once you've built MI there's no reason
whatsoever you couldn't teach it English as well.</span></font><o:p></o:p><=
/p>

<p><font size=3D2 face=3DArial><span style=3D'font-size:10.0pt;font-family:=
Arial'>The
problem with human languages is that they are based on understanding.&nbsp;=
 The
problem with computer languages is that they're based on digital principles
(which don't really apply in an analog world).</span></font><o:p></o:p></p>

